---
import { ROCK_MATERIALS } from '../scripts/materials-data.js';

const materialPages = {
  'pea-gravel': '/pea-gravel-calculator',
  'river-rock-1': '/river-rock-calculator',
  'crushed-limestone': '/crushed-stone-calculator',
  'crushed-granite': '/crushed-granite-calculator',
  'decomposed-granite': '/decomposed-granite-calculator',
  'lava-rock': '/lava-rock-calculator',
  'marble-chips': '/marble-chips-calculator',
  'river-rock-3-5': '/river-rock-calculator',
  'quarry-process': '/crusher-run-calculator',
  'rip-rap': '/riprap-calculator',
  'egg-rock': '/egg-rock-calculator',
  'clean-stone-57': '/drain-rock-calculator',
  'crusher-run-21a': '/crusher-run-calculator',
  'base-gravel-21a': '/driveway-rock-calculator'
};

const coverageAt = (material, depth) => material.minDepthInches > depth
  ? '—'
  : Math.round(324 / (depth * material.tonsPerCuYd)).toLocaleString('en-US');
---

<article class="home-guide" id="rock-coverage-guide">
  <div class="container">
    <header class="guide-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">From measurements to an order</p>
      <h2>How much rock will cover your space?</h2>
      <p>Start with volume. Area and finished depth tell you the cubic yards; the selected rock's loose bulk density converts that volume to an estimated weight. One cubic yard covers <strong>108 square feet at 3 inches</strong> before extra material.</p>
      <p>Need a worked measurement example? Read <a href="/how-much-rock-do-i-need">how much rock to order</a>. For a simpler single-bed tool, use the <a href="/rock-calculator">rock calculator</a>; compare project-specific layer depths in the <a href="/rock-depth-guide">depth guide</a>.</p>
    </header>

    <section class="example-card" aria-labelledby="example-title">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">A 10 × 10 foot bed, 3 inches deep</p>
        <h3 id="example-title">What the default pea gravel estimate means</h3>
        <p>The measured bed needs 25 cubic feet, or 0.93 cubic yard. With the calculator's selected 10% extra allowance, plan for <strong>1.02 cubic yards, about 1.38 short tons, or 55 bags of 0.5 cubic foot</strong>. The tonnage uses the current pea gravel planning factor of 1.35 tons per cubic yard. Confirm the supplier's actual density and bag yield before ordering.</p>
        <a class="text-link" href="#calculator">Try your own measurements →</a>
      </div>
      <figure>
        <img src="/rock-volume-diagram.svg" width="900" height="420" loading="lazy" alt="Diagram of a 10 by 10 foot rock bed with a 3 inch finished layer: 100 square feet times 3 inches equals 25 cubic feet or 0.93 cubic yard before extra material." />
        <figcaption>Area sets the footprint; depth creates the volume. Diagram is illustrative, not to scale.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </section>

    <section class="guide-section" id="mathematical-derivation">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">The calculation</p>
        <h2>Turn square feet and inches into yards, tons, and bags</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="formula-grid">
        <div class="formula-card"><span class="step">01 · Volume</span><strong>Cubic yards = square feet × depth in inches ÷ 324</strong><p>The divisor combines 12 inches per foot with 27 cubic feet per cubic yard. For a circle or ring, the calculator finds its area first.</p></div>
        <div class="formula-card"><span class="step">02 · Weight</span><strong>Short tons = cubic yards × tons per cubic yard</strong><p>Loose bulk density varies by material, gradation, packing, and moisture. Use the supplier's value when available.</p></div>
        <div class="formula-card"><span class="step">03 · Bags</span><strong>0.5 ft³ bags = cubic yards × 54</strong><p>Round up to whole bags. The tool assumes each bag contains 0.5 cubic foot; check the volume printed on the product you buy.</p></div>
      </div>
      <p class="guide-note">The calculator applies your extra material allowance after finding the finished layer volume. It changes order volume, tons, and bag count together. A density change affects <em>weight</em>, while the same volume still needs the same number of volume-labeled bags.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="guide-section" id="density-reference-table">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">Choose the material</p>
        <h2>Rock weight and coverage by material</h2>
        <p>These are the same planning factors used by the calculator above. Coverage per ton is geometric and excludes its extra material allowance. A dash means the stated depth is thinner than this tool's minimum suggested layer for that stone size.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Rock density and coverage table" tabindex="0">
        <table>
          <caption>Current calculator defaults, not supplier specifications</caption>
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Rock</th><th scope="col">Typical size</th><th scope="col">Tons/yd³</th><th scope="col">Sq ft/ton at 2 in</th><th scope="col">Sq ft/ton at 3 in</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {ROCK_MATERIALS.map((material) => (
              <tr>
                <th scope="row"><a href={materialPages[material.id]}>{material.name}</a></th>
                <td>{material.typicalSize}</td>
                <td>{material.tonsPerCuYd.toFixed(3).replace(/0+$/, '').replace(/\.$/, '')}</td>
                <td>{coverageAt(material, 2)}</td>
                <td>{coverageAt(material, 3)}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="table-hint">Swipe or scroll sideways to see every column. If your supplier gives a loose bulk density in pounds per cubic yard, enter it under Advanced Density and Compaction for a more useful ton estimate.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="guide-section" id="depth-guidelines">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">Match depth to the job</p>
        <h2>Calculate each layer for its actual purpose</h2>
        <p>A decorative top layer, compacted driveway base, and drainage trench solve different problems. Enter the <strong>finished depth of one layer</strong>, then run another calculation if the project also has a base.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="decision-grid">
        <div class="decision-card"><h3>Garden beds</h3><p>Start around 2–3 inches for small decorative stone, then adjust for particle size and the look you want. Rock can collect airborne weed seeds and warm the soil; it does not make a planting bed maintenance free.</p><a href="/landscape-rock-calculator">Explore landscape rock →</a></div>
        <div class="decision-card"><h3>Walkways</h3><p>For a walkable surface, distinguish the visible stone from any prepared base. Rounded <a href="/pea-gravel-calculator">pea gravel</a> moves underfoot; compactable <a href="/decomposed-granite-calculator">decomposed granite</a> behaves differently.</p><a href="/rock-depth-guide">Compare layer depths →</a></div>
        <div class="decision-card"><h3>Driveways</h3><p>Use the driveway plan's separate base and surface thicknesses. Local soil, traffic, drainage, and aggregate specification decide the build; one universal depth will not fit every site.</p><a href="/driveway-rock-calculator">Plan a driveway →</a></div>
        <div class="decision-card"><h3>Drainage and creek beds</h3><p>Stone size, channel shape, pipe, and drainage design change the quantity. For a pipe trench, subtract pipe displacement and use the specified washed aggregate.</p><a href="/drain-rock-calculator">Estimate drain rock →</a></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="guide-section split-section" id="angular-vs-rounded">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Shape changes performance</p>
        <h2>Angular stone and rounded rock are not interchangeable</h2>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p>Angular crushed stone can interlock when appropriately graded and compacted. Smooth river rock rolls and shifts more easily, making it better suited to decorative beds than a vehicle surface. Clean stone has open spaces for drainage; dense graded base contains fines that help it bind. The right choice depends on the project, not just its color.</p>
        <a class="text-link" href="/angular-vs-rounded-rock">Compare particle shapes →</a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="guide-section split-section" id="compaction-factors">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Allowances explained</p>
        <h2>Use extra material and compaction deliberately</h2>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p>The default 10% is an adjustable planning cushion for low spots and measurement error, not a guaranteed requirement. If you are estimating a compacted layer of crusher run or other material with fines, the Advanced Controls can apply an additional loose-to-finished volume factor. Keep that factor separate from the ordinary extra allowance, and confirm the target compacted thickness with the project's specification or supplier.</p>
        <a class="text-link" href="/crusher-run-calculator">See compactable base material →</a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="guide-section" id="bulk-vs-bags">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">Before checkout</p>
        <h2>Buy by the unit the seller actually uses</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="buying-grid">
        <div><h3>Bags</h3><p>Convenient for a small patch or a location with difficult access. One cubic yard equals 54 half-cubic-foot bags; 20 such bags equal only 0.37 cubic yard. Bag volumes vary, so compare cubic feet rather than counting bags alone.</p></div>
        <div><h3>Bulk by yard or ton</h3><p>Ask for the actual material and gradation, its loose tons-per-yard factor, order minimum, delivery fee, and where a truck can unload. A scale ticket reports weight; it establishes density only when the corresponding load volume is known.</p></div>
      </div>
      <p class="guide-note">Compare the delivered total for your project. Vehicle payload and access limits vary; check the vehicle rating and supplier loading policy before choosing pickup.</p>
      <a class="text-link" href="/bulk-vs-bagged-rock">Compare bulk and bagged rock →</a>
    </section>

    <section class="guide-section split-section" id="installation-protocol">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Prepare the site</p>
        <h2>The calculator estimates material, not the whole installation</h2>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p>Mark the boundary, measure in sections, check finished grade and drainage, and plan how the stone will be contained. Uneven soil can use more material than a flat rectangle suggests. Calculate edging, fabric, base, and visible stone separately where your design calls for them.</p>
        <a class="text-link" href="/how-to-install-landscape-rock">Read the installation guide →</a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="guide-section" id="calculation-pitfalls">
      <div class="section-heading"><p class="eyebrow">Final order check</p><h2>Four details worth confirming</h2></div>
      <ol class="checklist">
        <li>Measure the actual footprint, including curves or separate beds. Do not mistake a radius for a diameter.</li>
        <li>Enter the finished stone depth, and estimate a compacted base as a separate material layer.</li>
        <li>Match the seller's unit: cubic yards, short tons, or bags with a stated cubic-foot yield.</li>
        <li>Ask for the supplier's density, availability, delivery minimum, and space needed to unload.</li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section class="guide-section source-section" id="quarry-calibration">
      <h2>How the estimate is grounded</h2>
      <p>The volume math is geometry. The material densities are editable starting estimates, not measured values for a particular quarry load. <a href="https://dirtandgravel.psu.edu/general-resources/dglvr-materials-calculator/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Penn State's materials calculator</a> also advises using local supplier factors. <a href="https://store.astm.org/c0029_c0029m-23.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ASTM C29/C29M</a> describes a way to measure aggregate bulk density under defined conditions; naming the method does not certify this site's defaults. For bag sizing, check the manufacturer's published cubic-foot yield, such as <a href="https://pdf.lowes.com/productdocuments/3479d7f1-7cfc-43e0-8988-acf9bdb79b69/68529643.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SAKRETE's product data</a>.</p>
    </section>
  </div>
</article>

<style>
  .home-guide { padding: clamp(3rem, 6vw, 6rem) 0; color: var(--text-primary); }
  .home-guide .container { max-width: 1120px; }
  .guide-heading { max-width: 780px; margin-bottom: 2rem; }
  .eyebrow { color: var(--gold-500); font-size: .75rem; font-weight: 800; letter-spacing: .12em; text-transform: uppercase; margin: 0 0 .6rem; }
  h2 { font-size: clamp(1.6rem, 3vw, 2.35rem); line-height: 1.2; letter-spacing: -.025em; margin: 0 0 1rem; }
  h3 { font-size: 1.12rem; line-height: 1.35; margin: 0 0 .65rem; }
  p, li { color: var(--text-secondary); line-height: 1.75; }
  p { margin: 0 0 1rem; }
  strong { color: var(--text-primary); }
  a { color: var(--gold-500); }
  .text-link { display: inline-block; font-weight: 700; text-underline-offset: .2em; }
  .example-card { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: clamp(1.5rem, 4vw, 3.5rem); align-items: center; padding: clamp(1.25rem, 3vw, 2.5rem); background: var(--surface-raised); border: 1px solid var(--surface-glass-border); border-radius: var(--radius-lg); }
  figure { margin: 0; min-width: 0; }
  figure img { width: 100%; height: auto; display: block; border-radius: .75rem; }
  figcaption { color: var(--text-muted); font-size: .8rem; margin-top: .65rem; line-height: 1.4; }
  .guide-section { padding: clamp(2.5rem, 5vw, 4.5rem) 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--surface-glass-border); }
  .section-heading { max-width: 800px; margin-bottom: 1.5rem; }
  .formula-grid, .decision-grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: 1rem; }
  .formula-card, .decision-card, .buying-grid > div { background: var(--surface-raised); border: 1px solid var(--surface-glass-border); border-radius: var(--radius-md); padding: 1.35rem; }
  .formula-card .step { display: block; color: var(--gold-500); font-size: .75rem; font-weight: 800; letter-spacing: .08em; margin-bottom: .8rem; text-transform: uppercase; }
  .formula-card strong { display: block; font-size: 1rem; line-height: 1.4; margin-bottom: .65rem; }
  .formula-card p, .decision-card p, .buying-grid p { margin-bottom: 0; }
  .guide-note { background: var(--gold-glow); border-left: 3px solid var(--gold-500); padding: 1rem 1.25rem; margin: 1.25rem 0 0; border-radius: .25rem; }
  .table-scroll { max-width: 100%; overflow-x: auto; overscroll-behavior-inline: contain; -webkit-overflow-scrolling: touch; border: 1px solid var(--surface-glass-border); border-radius: var(--radius-md); }
  table { width: 100%; min-width: 730px; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: .875rem; }
  caption { text-align: left; color: var(--text-secondary); font-weight: 700; padding: 1rem; }
  th, td { text-align: left; padding: .75rem 1rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--surface-glass-border); }
  thead th { color: var(--text-primary); background: var(--surface-raised); white-space: nowrap; }
  tbody th { font-weight: 600; }
  tbody tr:nth-child(even) { background: var(--surface-raised); }
  .table-hint { font-size: .85rem; margin: .7rem 0 0; }
  .decision-grid { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); }
  .decision-card a { display: inline-block; margin-top: .8rem; font-weight: 700; }
  .split-section { display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(220px, .7fr) minmax(0, 1fr); gap: clamp(1.5rem, 4vw, 4rem); }
  .buying-grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: 1rem; }
  .checklist { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: 1rem 2rem; padding-left: 1.35rem; margin: 0; }
  .checklist li { padding-left: .35rem; }
  .source-section { border: 0; padding-bottom: 0; max-width: 850px; }
  @media (max-width: 780px) { .example-card, .split-section { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } .formula-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } }
  @media (max-width: 600px) { .decision-grid, .buying-grid, .checklist { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } .home-guide { overflow-x: clip; } }
</style>
